==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{com|en|mile|stone}}. Compare {{cog|nl|mijlsteen|t=milestone}}, {{cog|nds-de|Mielensteen|t=milestone}}, {{cog|de|Meilenstein|t=milestone}}, {{cog|sv|milsten|t=milestone}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈmaɪlstəʊn/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-milestone.wav|Audio (Southern England)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A stone [[milepost]] (or by extension in other materials), one of a series of numbered markers placed along a road at regular intervals, typically at the side of the road or in a median.
# {{lb|en|idiomatic}} An [[important]] [[event]] in a person's [[life]] or [[career]], in the [[history]] of a [[nation]], in the life of some [[project]], etc.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1933|author=w:Stephen Spender|title=The Funeral
|passage=Death is another '''milestone''' on their way.}}
#* {{quote-journal
|1=en
|year=2012
|month=March-April
|author=[[w:Terry Sejnowski|Terrence J. Sejnowski]]
|title=Well-connected Brains
|volume=100
|issue=2
|page=171
|magazine=w:American Scientist
|url=http://www.americanscientist.org/bookshelf/pub/well-connected-brains
|passage=Creating a complete map of the human connectome would therefore be a monumental '''milestone''' but not the end of the journey to understanding how our brains work.
|accessdate=24 May 2013
|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20170427064626/http://www.americanscientist.org/bookshelf/pub/well-connected-brains
|archivedate=27 April 2017
}}
